Okay, fair enough. I was just reacting to this:

> you leverage underdeterminism for scattershot mutations.

...which sounds very much like selection to me, and also a statement of what biologists know to be true (i.e., random mutations happen all the time, and a small fraction of them have some kind of selective advantage). I understand the confusion, though.

> yes, because we are doing it.

I was specifically referring to this statement:

> you leverage underdeterminism for scattershot mutations.

I don't understand what you mean by this.

> yes, if we are doing it.

It's unclear to me how doing it would matter.

> I don't see the relevance of this distinction.

In what you are saying, I see two distinct processes:

1. We perform a random search and converge to a model that fits the data well.
2. Something converges to a model that fits the data well.

It's clear to me that the former is a form of inference, and that the latter is a form of evolution. So, how do they differ?

