I think we're trying to say the same thing, and we're just using different words for it.

It's probably easier to see this by going back to my earlier statement about random mutations having an expected effect. This is exactly what evolution does. Evolution is the random mutation of a DNA molecule. Selection is the process by which some of the mutants get an advantage. The net result of this process is that the DNA molecule is altered in a way that increases its fitness.

I've noticed that, when I say these things, a lot of people seem to assume that I am saying that evolution is some kind of mysterious intelligent process. But this isn't what I am saying. I am saying that evolution is a random process that happens to produce intelligent processes.

I'm not sure how else to describe this.

