how the emergence of a non-local and non-combinatorial memory in the network of loops allows for a non-local and non-combinatorial inference of a world (or of multiple worlds). my work here is to try to say how that works.

I’m reading Koch and Crick 1998, “Towards a cognitive neuroscience of consciousness.”

I haven’t found the relevant passage yet, but it does seem to be about the problem that is being addressed in loom: that a subject can have a non-local and non-combinatorial experience, a memory of a place you haven’t been, a word you’ve never heard, a thing you’ve never seen. I think this is about the emergence of memory and world-building in the neural network, and I think that it is a good place to start talking about how loom does memory and world-building in the neural network.

